"""A dev install's function, run on this computer for your own actions.

``workpeg project serve <peg> --fake-cloud`` serves a peg's dev page and runs
its ``function/`` folder; ``workpeg ui serve <ui> --fake-cloud --listen
[FUNCTION_DIR]`` does the same for a UI folder. There is no ``workpeg
function listen`` (owner, 2026-11-11): the runner starts from the command
that serves the page.

WHAT RUNS WHERE

When you save a row in a hooked table of your dev install, the API queues
the call for you, and only for you. This runner collects it from the public
API, with your own ``WORKPEG_PK``, runs your function here, and posts the
answer back. Nothing of yours runs on Workpeg, the API never opens a
connection to this computer, and a teammate's save runs on their computer or
not at all. The wire is ``docs/dev_functions_wire.md``.

THE KEY

``WORKPEG_PK`` is read from the environment, never from a flag, and is sent
only to :data:`API_BASE` with no redirect followed, no proxy, no cookie and
no ``Origin``. A child running as the same user can read its parent's
environment block (``/proc/<ppid>/environ`` on Linux, ``KERN_PROCARGS2`` on
macOS), so scrubbing the function's environment is not enough: the command
re-executes itself with the variable removed and the key handed over an
inherited pipe (:func:`reexec_without_key`, :func:`take_key`), and holds it
in memory only. That narrows what a function can find; in process mode it
still runs as you, with everything you can read.

DOCKER BY DEFAULT

``--with docker`` builds the function's own Dockerfile as
``workpeg-listen-<namespace>:dev``, never as its ``build.image``, and runs
it with ``--rm``, published on ``127.0.0.1`` only, behind a random invoke
key (``WORKPEG_INVOKE_KEY``): the container sees the envelope and that key,
and nothing of yours. Before the first call the runner checks that the
container refuses a call without that key, and stops one built FROM a base
too old to. ``--with process`` runs each call as ``python -m
workpeg.runtime`` in the function's folder, opening no port; it is for code
you wrote yourself.

WHAT IT PRINTS

The SDK's own words, and every string from the API, from ``docker build``
and from the function through :func:`workpeg.terminal.shown`, with the
call's token and the key replaced by ``<token>`` and ``<key>``, so a table
name cannot drive the terminal and no credential reaches a log. Under
``--debug`` alone the function's child has this terminal itself, so that
``breakpoint()`` works: what that child prints reaches the terminal as it
is, unescaped and unmasked, and only its answer passes through here.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

